<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_em-waves" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>Electromagnetic Waves</title>

	<assemblage component="implementation">
  <title>Implementation Notes</title>
  <p>
	The activities in this section were designed to be implemented in a 50-minute classroom, after students have completed the previous sections in the chapter.
	</p>
  </assemblage>

  <subsubsection>
	<title>Activities</title>
	
	<activity>
	<title>Electromagnetism in a Vacuum</title>
	<introduction>
		<p>
	
	</p>
	<p>
		Suppose you want to find out what sort of electric and magnetic fields could exist in a vacuum (no matter, no charges, no currents).
	</p>
	<p>
		For the sake of argument, assume <m>E</m> points only in the <m>x</m>-direction and <m>B</m> points only in the <m>y</m>-direction, as in the figure below.  You want to know how <m>E</m> and <m>B</m> change in space and time!
	</p>

	<figure xml:id="fig_EMWave0">
	<title>EM Vacuum Figure 0</title>
	<caption>Ex and By.</caption>
	<image source="EMWave0.png" width = "50%"><description><p>A 3D Cartesian coordinate system with z to the right, x upward, and y out of the page.  E points in the x direction and B points in the y direction.</p></description></image>
	</figure>

	</introduction>
	
		<task><statement>
			<p>
				Use Gauss's Law to argue that <m>E_{top} = E_{bottom}</m> for the cube in the figure below (assume it has side length <m>s</m>).
			</p>
			<figure xml:id="fig_EMWave1">
			<title>EM Vacuum Figure 1</title>
			<caption>A cube for using Gauss's Law.</caption>
			<image source="EMWave1.png" width = "50%"><description><p>A 3D Cartesian coordinate system with z to the right, x upward, and y out of the page.  On a cubical surface, Etop points up at the top of the cube and Ebottom points up at the bottom of the cube.</p></description></image>
			</figure>
		</statement></task>
		<task><statement>
			<p>
				Use Gauss's Law to argue that <m>B_{back} = B_{front}</m> for the cube in the figure below (assume it has side length <m>s</m>).
			</p>
			<figure xml:id="fig_EMWave2">
			<title>EM Vacuum Figure 2</title>
			<caption>A cube for using Gauss's Law.</caption>
			<image source="EMWave2.png" width = "50%"><description><p>A 3D Cartesian coordinate system with z to the right, x upward, and y out of the page.  On a cubical surface, Bback points out at the back of the cube and Bfront points out at the front of the cube.</p></description></image>
			</figure>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				<md>
					E_{right}s - E_{left}s = -s^2 \frac{\partial B}{\partial t}
				</md>
				<md>
					\frac{E_{right} - E_{left}}{s} = - \frac{\partial B}{\partial t}
				</md>
				<md>
					\frac{\partial E_x}{\partial z} = - \frac{\partial B_y}{\partial t}
				</md>
				The last step is made by assuming the size of the cube <m>s \rightarrow dz</m> is infinitesimal.
			</p>
		</solution>
		</task>
		<task><statement>
			<p>
				Use Faraday's Law to relate the electric fields to the magnetic field for the highlighted loop in the figure below (assume it has side length <m>s</m>).
			</p>
			<figure xml:id="fig_EMWave3">
			<title>EM Vacuum Figure 3</title>
			<caption>A cube for using Faraday's Law.</caption>
			<image source="EMWave3.png" width = "50%"><description><p>A 3D Cartesian coordinate system with z to the right, x upward, and y out of the page.  The front face of a cubical surface is highlighted, with B pointing outward, Eleft pointing upward along the left edge, and Eright pointing downward along the right edge.</p></description></image>
			</figure>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				<md>
					B_{left}s - B_{right}s = \mu_0 \epsilon_0 s^2 \frac{\partial E}{\partial t}
				</md>
				<md>
					\frac{B_{left} - B_{right}}{s} = \mu_0 \epsilon_0 \frac{\partial E}{\partial t}
				</md>
				<md>
					- \frac{\partial B_y}{\partial z} = \mu_0 \epsilon_0 \frac{\partial E_x}{\partial t}
				</md>
				The last step is made by assuming the size of the cube <m>s \rightarrow dz</m> is infinitesimal.
			</p>
		</solution>
		</task>
		<task><statement>
			<p>
				Use the Ampere-Maxwell Law to relate the magnetic fields to the electric field for the highlighted loop in the figure below (assume it has side length <m>s</m>).
			</p>
			<figure xml:id="fig_EMWave4">
			<title>EM Vacuum Figure 4</title>
			<caption>A cube for using the Ampère-Maxwell Law.</caption>
			<image source="EMWave4.png" width = "50%"><description><p>A 3D Cartesian coordinate system with z to the right, x upward, and y out of the page.  The top face of a cubical surface is highlighted, with E pointing upward, Bleft pointing outward along the left edge, and Bright pointing outward along the right edge.</p></description></image>
			</figure>
		</statement></task>
		<task><statement>
			<p>
				Combining the above results gives the following equation:
				<md>
					\frac{\partial^2 E_x}{\partial z^2} = \mu_0 \epsilon_0 \frac{\partial^2 E_x}{\partial t^2}
				</md>
				This is a <xref ref="def_wave"/> equation!  Use this to calculate the speed of an electromagnetic wave.
			</p>
		</statement>
	<solution>
		<p>
			<md>
				\epsilon_{0} = 8.85 \times 10^{-12} \frac{\mathrm{C^2}}{\mathrm{Nm^2}}
			</md>
			<md>
				\mu_0 = 4\pi \times 10^{-7} \mathrm{~\frac{Tm}{A}}
			</md>
			<md>
				c = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\mu_0\epsilon_0}} \approx 3 \times 10^8 \mathrm{~m/s}
			</md>
			
		</p>
	</solution></task>
</activity>

	<assumption xml:id="a_plane-waves">
		<title>Electromagnetic Plane Wave</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>
				An <alert>electromagnetic plane wave</alert> is a sinusoidal traveling wave in which the electric and magnetic fields oscillate in time and space.  The example wave below is traveling in the positive <m>z</m>-direction.
				<md>
					\vec{E}(x, y, z, t) = \left( E_o \hat{x} \right) \cos\left(\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}z - 2\pi ft + \phi_i\right)
				</md>
				<md>
					\vec{B}(x, y, z, t) = \left( B_o \hat{y} \right) \cos\left(\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}z - 2\pi ft + \phi_i\right)
				</md>
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>Electromagnetic waves are transverse.</alert>  Both the direction of the electric field and the direction of the magnetic field are perpendicular to the direction of travel.  The electric and magnetic fields are also mutually perpendicular.  The wave always travels in a direction given by <m>\vec{E} \times \vec{B}</m>.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>Plane waves are formed from uniform planes.</alert>  For example, <m>\vec{E}</m> and <m>\vec{B}</m> in the equations above do not depend on <m>x</m> or <m>y</m>, so the fields are <em>the same</em> throughout any plane of constant <m>z</m>.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>Electromagnetic waves travel at the speed of light.</alert>  In a vacuum, <m>c = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\mu_0\epsilon_0}} \approx 3 \times 10^8 \mathrm{~m/s}</m>.  As with other waves, the frequency and wavelength are related by the wave speed using: <m>c = f\lambda</m>.  
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>The electric and magnetic fields are tied together.</alert>  The wave cannot exist without both.  The fields are <em>in phase</em> with each other, and their magnitudes are related by <m>B_o = E_o/c</m>.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ul>
			<figure xml:id="fig_EMWave">
				<title>Electromagnetic Plane Wave Diagram</title>
				<caption>The plane wave solution of Maxwell's equations has the B field directly proportional to the <m>E</m> field at each point, with the relative directions shown.</caption>
				<image source="EMWave.png" width = "100%"><description><p>Figure shows the positive x direction as the direction of propagation. The positive y direction is labeled electric field and the positive z direction is labeled magnetic field. A sine wave in the xy plane is labeled E. The electric field arrows have their bases on the x axis and their tips on wave E. Another sine wave labeled B is in the xz plane. The magnetic field arrows have their bases on the x axis and their tips on wave B. Waves E and B have the same wavelength and are in phase with each other.</p></description></image>
				</figure>
		</statement>
	</assumption>


	<references>
		<biblio>
			<p>
				EM Wave Figure from <url href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/16-2-plane-electromagnetic-waves">University Physics Volume 2</url>. OpenStax.
			</p>
		</biblio>
	</references>

</subsubsection>
	
<!--NO AI-->
